    "intentType": "evaluative",
    "shortAnswer": "A good net interest margin (NIM) depends on the type of bank. Community banks typically fall between 3.50% and 4.50%, regional banks between 3.00% and 3.75%, and large national banks between 2.00% and 3.00%. The US industry average has historically run around 3.0% to 3.5%.",
    "fullAnswer": "Net interest margin (NIM) varies significantly by bank type, so there is no single number that qualifies as 'good' across the industry. The most useful approach is to benchmark a bank's NIM against a peer group of similar institutions.\n\nUS commercial banks as a whole have historically averaged NIM between 3.0% and 3.5%, based on FDIC aggregate data. That average masks substantial variation:\n\n- Community banks focused on relationship commercial lending with strong core deposit franchises often report NIMs of 3.50% to 4.50%\n\n- Regional banks typically fall in the 3.00% to 3.75% range\n\n- Large money center banks, whose asset bases include more low-yield trading assets and wholesale lending, commonly report NIMs of 2.00% to 3.00%\n\n## Why These Ranges Differ So Much\n\nThese gaps reflect structural differences in how banks operate, not necessarily differences in management quality. A community bank in a market with limited competition can price loans at wider spreads and fund them with low-cost local deposits. A money center bank operating in competitive wholesale markets faces narrower lending spreads and more rate-sensitive funding.\n\nThe deposit side of the balance sheet is often the biggest differentiator. A bank funding 35% of its assets with non-interest-bearing checking accounts has a permanent cost advantage over one funding only 10% with non-interest-bearing sources. That advantage flows directly into NIM regardless of what the bank does on the lending side.\n\n## Evaluating NIM Within a Peer Group\n\nNIM differences become much more informative when you compare banks of similar size, geography, and business focus. Within a well-matched peer group, a bank running a 3.80% NIM versus peers averaging 3.30% may have several advantages:\n\n- A stronger deposit franchise with more non-interest-bearing deposits\n\n- More disciplined loan pricing\n\n- A higher-yielding loan mix weighted toward commercial real estate or small business lending\n\n- Some combination of these factors\n\nThe catch is that some of these advantages come with tradeoffs. Higher-yielding loan categories tend to carry more credit risk. A bank with the widest NIM in its peer group might be earning those extra basis points by concentrating in riskier loan types. Checking asset quality metrics (non-performing loan ratios, net charge-off rates) alongside NIM helps separate genuine franchise strength from risk-taking that inflates the margin.\n\nNIM trends over several quarters can reveal more than a single snapshot. A bank that maintains its NIM within a tight range through different rate environments is likely managing its balance sheet well. One whose NIM swings sharply from quarter to quarter may have duration mismatches or an unstable funding base.\n\n## How Interest Rates Shift the Target\n\nThe interest rate environment shifts NIM levels across the entire industry, which means what counts as a strong NIM changes over time. Steep yield curves (where long-term rates sit well above short-term rates) tend to produce wider NIMs industry-wide because banks fund with short-duration deposits and lend at longer durations. Flat or inverted yield curves compress NIM even at well-managed banks.\n\nRapidly rising rate environments create a transitional effect worth understanding. Deposit costs often lag at first, because banks don't immediately raise rates on existing deposits. This lag temporarily boosts NIM.\n\nBut as competition for deposits intensifies and customers shift money into higher-yielding products, deposit costs catch up and the NIM benefit fades. The reverse happens when rates fall quickly.\n\nBecause of these cyclical swings, comparing a bank's NIM to its own history over a full rate cycle gives better context than comparing against a fixed benchmark. A bank that consistently sits in the top quartile of its peer group through both rising and falling rate environments is demonstrating real franchise quality.\n\n## NIM and Bottom-Line Profitability\n\nA wide NIM alone does not guarantee strong profitability. A bank earning a 4.00% NIM but running a 75% efficiency ratio converts much less of that spread into net income than a bank earning a 3.25% NIM with a 55% efficiency ratio. NIM combined with the efficiency ratio determines how much of the interest margin actually reaches the bottom line, and this combined effect is one of the strongest drivers of return on average assets (ROAA).\n\nFor this reason, screening for banks with above-average NIM and below-average efficiency ratios within a peer group is one of the more reliable ways to identify strong core earnings franchises.",
