    "slug": "how-to-calculate-texas-ratio",
    "question": "How do I calculate the Texas Ratio?",
    "cluster": "capital-and-risk",
    "clusterName": "Capital Strength and Asset Quality",
    "intentType": "procedural",
    "shortAnswer": "The Texas Ratio is calculated by dividing non-performing assets by the sum of tangible common equity and the allowance for credit losses, measuring whether a bank has sufficient capital and reserves to absorb its problem assets",
    "fullAnswer": "The formula for the Texas Ratio is:\n\nTexas Ratio = Non-Performing Assets / (Tangible Common Equity + Allowance for Credit Losses)\n\nThe result is expressed as a percentage.\n\nStep-by-step calculation:\n\n1. Calculate non-performing assets: non-performing loans (non-accrual + 90+ days past due) plus OREO plus other foreclosed assets.\n2. Calculate tangible common equity: total shareholders' equity minus goodwill, minus other intangible assets, minus preferred stock.\n3. Find the allowance for credit losses on the balance sheet.\n4. Add tangible common equity and the allowance for credit losses. This sum represents the total loss-absorbing capacity available to cover problem assets.\n5. Divide non-performing assets by the sum from step 4.\n\nWorked example: A bank has $67 million in non-performing assets (from the NPA calculation). Tangible common equity is $520 million (total equity of $600M minus $70M goodwill minus $10M intangibles, no preferred stock). The allowance for credit losses is $55 million. Texas Ratio = $67M / ($520M + $55M) = $67M / $575M = 11.7%.\n\nWhy 100% is the critical threshold: A Texas Ratio above 100% means the bank's non-performing assets exceed its combined tangible equity and reserves. At this level, if the bank were forced to write off all its problem assets immediately, it would not have enough capital and reserves to absorb the losses without becoming insolvent. Historically, Texas Ratios above 100% have been associated with significantly elevated failure risk, particularly when the ratio is rising and problem assets are not being resolved.\n\nUsing tangible common equity vs total equity: The standard Texas Ratio formula uses tangible common equity because goodwill and intangible assets cannot absorb losses. Some simplified versions use total equity in the denominator, which produces a lower (more favorable) ratio but is less conservative. For the most meaningful analysis, use the tangible version.\n\nAlternative denominator formulations: Some analysts use loan loss reserves (a synonym for the allowance for credit losses) in the denominator. Others use total capital and reserves. The most common formulation uses tangible common equity plus the allowance. Be aware of which version a source is using when comparing Texas Ratio figures across different analysts or data providers.\n\nThe Texas Ratio as a screening tool: The ratio was designed as a failure prediction tool and is most useful for quickly identifying banks under credit stress. Screening for banks with Texas Ratios above 50% identifies institutions where credit problems are consuming a meaningful share of the loss-absorbing cushion. Screening for ratios below 20% identifies banks where asset quality problems are well within manageable bounds.\n\nTrend matters more than a single reading: A Texas Ratio that has risen from 15% to 40% over four quarters is more concerning than a stable ratio at 45%. The trajectory indicates whether credit problems are growing or stabilizing.\n\nWhere to find the inputs: Non-performing asset data is in the credit quality disclosures. Total equity, goodwill, and intangibles are on the balance sheet. The allowance for credit losses is on the balance sheet. All inputs are available from 10-K, 10-Q, earnings releases, and FFIEC call report data.",
