    "slug": "community-vs-regional-vs-money-center",
    "question": "How do I compare a community bank to a regional bank to a money center bank?",
    "cluster": "bank-types",
    "clusterName": "Understanding Bank Types",
    "intentType": "comparative",
    "shortAnswer": "Community banks, regional banks, and money center banks differ primarily in asset size, geographic scope, and business model complexity, and each tier requires different analytical expectations when evaluating performance metrics",
    "fullAnswer": "The U.S. banking industry is commonly segmented into three broad tiers based on asset size and geographic reach, though the boundaries between them are not precisely defined and the terminology varies across sources.\n\nCommunity banks are the smallest tier, generally defined as institutions with total assets below $10 billion. The FDIC's formal research definition uses a more nuanced set of criteria that includes asset size, geographic footprint, and lending focus, but the $10 billion threshold is the most widely cited boundary in investor discussions. Community banks typically operate in a single metropolitan area or a cluster of rural counties. Their revenue is heavily concentrated in traditional spread banking: taking deposits and making loans, with net interest income accounting for 75% to 90% of total revenue. Community banks tend to have higher equity-to-assets ratios (often 9% to 12%) and higher net interest margins than larger banks, but also higher efficiency ratios due to limited scale.\n\nRegional banks occupy the middle tier, typically ranging from $10 billion to $100 billion in assets, though some definitions extend to $250 billion. These banks operate across multiple states or a broad multi-state region. They offer a wider array of products than community banks, often including wealth management, commercial real estate lending, treasury management services, and sometimes mortgage banking or insurance. Their revenue mix is more diversified, with non-interest income typically representing 20% to 35% of total revenue. Regional banks benefit from greater scale than community banks but face more complex regulatory requirements, particularly once they cross the $10 billion, $50 billion, and $100 billion asset thresholds that trigger progressively more stringent regulatory standards.\n\nMoney center banks are the largest institutions, typically with assets exceeding $250 billion. These banks operate nationally and often globally, with business lines that include investment banking, trading, capital markets, global transaction processing, credit cards, and asset management alongside traditional commercial banking. Their revenue mix is the most diversified, with non-interest income often accounting for 40% to 60% of total revenue. Money center banks generally report lower net interest margins than smaller banks but achieve competitive returns through volume, fee income, and operating leverage.\n\nWhen comparing banks across these tiers, the most important adjustment is to set appropriate benchmark expectations. A 4.00% net interest margin at a community bank is typical, while the same NIM at a money center bank would be extraordinary. A 65% efficiency ratio might be unremarkable for a small community bank but would signal cost problems at a large regional bank. BankSift's peer comparison features allow filtering by asset size to ensure you are evaluating each bank against relevant peers rather than against the industry as a whole.",
