    "slug": "national-vs-state-chartered-bank",
    "question": "What is the difference between a national bank and a state-chartered bank?",
    "cluster": "bank-types",
    "clusterName": "Understanding Bank Types",
    "intentType": "comparative",
    "shortAnswer": "A national bank receives its charter from the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency and operates under federal banking law, while a state-chartered bank receives its charter from a state banking department and operates under that state's banking law, though both types are subject to federal regulation",
    "fullAnswer": "The United States has a dual banking system in which banks can be chartered (authorized to operate) by either the federal government or a state government. This charter choice determines the bank's primary regulator but does not limit where the bank can do business or what products it can offer.\n\nNational banks are chartered by the Office of the Comptroller of the Currency (OCC), a bureau of the U.S. Department of the Treasury. National banks are required to include \"National\" or \"N.A.\" (National Association) in their name, which is a useful identifier when researching a bank. The OCC serves as the primary supervisor, conducting examinations and enforcing compliance with federal banking laws. National banks are automatically members of the Federal Reserve System and must carry FDIC deposit insurance.\n\nState-chartered banks receive their charter from the banking department of the state in which they are headquartered. Each state has its own banking laws and regulatory framework. State-chartered banks may choose whether to become members of the Federal Reserve System. Those that do (\"state member banks\") have the Federal Reserve as their primary federal regulator. Those that do not (\"state non-member banks\") have the FDIC as their primary federal regulator. Both types must carry FDIC insurance.\n\nFor bank stock investors, the charter type has limited direct impact on financial performance. National banks and state-chartered banks compete in the same markets, offer the same products, and are subject to the same capital requirements. The practical differences arise mainly in regulatory examination processes, branching rules in certain states, and the specific legal framework governing certain activities.\n\nWhere charter type becomes more relevant is in the regulatory capital and examination context. The OCC's examination approach, enforcement philosophy, and interpretive guidance may differ from those of state regulators or the FDIC. Banks occasionally switch charters (converting from state to national or vice versa) for strategic reasons, including differences in how regulators treat certain activities, branching rules, or examination approaches. These conversions are disclosed in SEC filings and regulatory announcements.\n\nWhen analyzing a bank, the primary regulator is listed in the bank's Call Report and on the FDIC's BankFind database. Knowing the primary regulator can help investors locate relevant enforcement actions, examination reports (for failed banks), and regulatory guidance that may affect the bank.",
