    "slug": "evaluating-cre-exposure",
    "question": "How do I evaluate a bank's commercial real estate (CRE) loan exposure?",
    "cluster": "advanced",
    "clusterName": "Advanced Bank Analysis",
    "intentType": "strategic",
    "shortAnswer": "Evaluating CRE exposure requires examining the bank's CRE concentration as a percentage of total capital and total loans, the composition by property type, geographic diversification, and the regulatory guidance that flags elevated concentration risk",
    "fullAnswer": "Commercial real estate lending is one of the most significant risk concentrations in community and regional banking. CRE loans include loans secured by office buildings, retail centers, multifamily apartment complexes, industrial properties, hotels, and construction and development projects. For many community banks, CRE loans represent 40% to 60% or more of the total loan portfolio, making CRE credit quality a primary determinant of the bank's overall financial health.\n\nThe starting point for evaluating CRE exposure is the concentration ratio. Federal banking regulators issued interagency guidance establishing two key thresholds: total CRE loans (excluding owner-occupied properties) exceeding 300% of the bank's total risk-based capital, and construction and development loans exceeding 100% of total risk-based capital. Banks that exceed either threshold are expected to maintain enhanced risk management practices, including board-approved concentration limits, stress testing of the CRE portfolio, and enhanced market analysis. Exceeding these thresholds does not prohibit the bank from making additional CRE loans, but it does invite heightened regulatory scrutiny during examinations.\n\nBeyond the aggregate concentration ratio, the composition of the CRE portfolio matters. Different property types carry different risk profiles. Multifamily lending (apartment complexes) has historically experienced lower loss rates than other CRE categories due to consistent housing demand. Retail-oriented CRE has faced structural headwinds from e-commerce. Office properties have experienced elevated vacancy rates in some markets. Construction and development (C&D) loans carry the highest risk because the collateral is not yet producing income and the bank is exposed to completion risk, market timing risk, and the borrower's ability to lease or sell the finished project.\n\nGeographic concentration compounds CRE risk. A community bank with 50% of its loans in CRE, all within a single metropolitan market, faces meaningful event risk if that market experiences an economic downturn, a major employer closure, or a sustained decline in property values. Diversification across markets and property types reduces the probability that a single adverse event will cause material losses across the portfolio.\n\nThe bank's 10-K filing and Call Report provide the data needed for this analysis. The Call Report's Schedule RC-C breaks down loans by type, including specific CRE categories. The 10-K's loan portfolio discussion typically provides additional detail on property types, geographic distribution, and loan-to-value ratios at origination. The bank's allowance for credit losses and specific reserves allocated to CRE segments indicate how management assesses the risk in this portfolio.\n\nWhen screening bank stocks, investors who want to avoid elevated CRE concentration risk can filter for banks with CRE-to-capital ratios below 300% and C&D-to-capital ratios below 100%. Those comfortable with CRE concentration can use these metrics to identify banks whose CRE expertise is a competitive advantage, provided the bank's underwriting history and credit metrics support that assessment.",
