    "slug": "what-is-aoci",
    "question": "What is accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI) and why does it matter for banks?",
    "cluster": "advanced",
    "clusterName": "Advanced Bank Analysis",
    "intentType": "definitional",
    "shortAnswer": "AOCI is a component of shareholders' equity that captures unrealized gains and losses, primarily from available-for-sale securities, that bypass the income statement, and it matters because large negative AOCI can significantly reduce a bank's book value and tangible book value",
    "fullAnswer": "Accumulated other comprehensive income (AOCI) is a line item within shareholders' equity on the balance sheet that records cumulative gains and losses from certain items that are excluded from net income on the income statement. For banks, the most significant component of AOCI is unrealized gains and losses on available-for-sale (AFS) investment securities.\n\nWhen a bank holds AFS securities, changes in their fair value are not recorded as gains or losses on the income statement (unless the securities are sold or deemed impaired). Instead, the unrealized change flows through other comprehensive income (OCI) on the statement of comprehensive income and accumulates in the AOCI line within equity. When market interest rates rise, the fair value of existing fixed-rate bonds falls, creating unrealized losses that reduce AOCI and therefore reduce total shareholders' equity. When rates fall, the reverse occurs.\n\nThe impact of AOCI on bank balance sheets became particularly visible during periods of rapid interest rate increases. Banks that had purchased long-duration bonds during low-rate periods saw the market value of those securities decline substantially, creating large negative AOCI balances. For some banks, the unrealized losses in AOCI were large enough to reduce tangible equity by 20% to 40% compared to what it would have been without the mark-to-market adjustment.\n\nAOCI affects valuation metrics directly. Book value per share and tangible book value per share both include AOCI. When AOCI is significantly negative, these metrics are depressed, which can make a bank appear more expensive on a price-to-book or price-to-tangible-book basis than its underlying franchise warrants. Some analysts adjust for AOCI by adding back the after-tax unrealized loss to calculate an \"adjusted\" tangible book value, though this approach assumes the bank will hold the securities to maturity and the losses will never be realized.\n\nThe regulatory treatment of AOCI varies by bank size. Under the U.S. regulatory capital framework, most community banks (those that are not \"advanced approaches\" institutions) have opted to exclude AOCI from their regulatory capital calculations. This means that unrealized securities losses do not reduce their regulatory capital ratios, even though they do reduce GAAP equity. Larger banks subject to the standardized approach generally include AOCI in regulatory capital, meaning unrealized losses directly reduce their CET1 and other capital ratios.\n\nOther components of AOCI for banks can include unrealized gains and losses on cash flow hedges, pension and other post-retirement benefit adjustments, and foreign currency translation adjustments (for banks with international operations). For most community and regional banks, the AFS securities component dominates.\n\nInvestors should monitor the AOCI balance and its trend over time, particularly during periods of changing interest rates. A bank with a large negative AOCI balance faces a decision: hold the securities to maturity and let the unrealized losses amortize away over time, or sell the securities (realizing the loss on the income statement) and reinvest at higher current yields.",
